course: dictionary
lesson: 15942
title: Un- words and sharing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word unshared is made of two parts. Un- means not, and shared means given to or used by others too. So unshared means not shared with anyone else, kept by one person only. «Mia's diary is unshared, so nobody else has read it.»
explain: Look at the words around it to check. «Ben's bedroom is unshared, so he does not have to take turns with his sister.» The clue is take turns: if a room is shared, two people use it, so unshared means only one person does.
explain: Unshared can also describe a feeling or a secret. «Sam carried an unshared worry all week and told no one.» The words told no one are the clue: Sam kept the worry inside, alone.
fact: Un- at the start of a word often means not, so unshared means not shared.
fact: «Unshared» means not shared with anyone else; kept by one person only.
fact: Computers have unshared files and folders that only you can see.
fact: «Unshared» can describe a secret, a room, a toy or a feeling that one person keeps alone.
fact: Some things should stay unshared, such as passwords: never tell yours to anyone.
exercise: «Mia's diary is unshared, so nobody else has read it.» What does unshared mean here? | Lost forever | Shared by the whole class | Written in pencil | *Not shared with anyone else
exercise: «Ben's bedroom is unshared, so he does not have to take turns with his sister.» What is true about Ben's room? | *Only Ben uses it | Ben and his sister use it together | Nobody is allowed in it | It is very big
exercise: «Sam carried an unshared worry all week and told no one.» Which word tells you the worry was kept private? | worry | week | *unshared | carried
test: «Your password is unshared, so never tell it to anyone.» What should you do with a password? | *Keep it unshared and private | Share it with all your friends | Post it online for others | Write it on the classroom wall
test: What does «unshared» mean? | Very loud in a noisy room | Shared by many people | Found on a beach | *Kept by one person only
exam: «The toy was unshared, so Lily played with it alone while her brother was at school.» What does unshared suggest? | *Only Lily used the toy | Lily gave the toy to her brother | Everyone played with the toy together | The toy was broken
